  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nA broad ridge covers the central Plains tonight/early this morning\nbetween a closed low over New England and a northern stream low\namplitude trough over Montana and the Dakotas. Beneath it, a\nsurface ridge is centered over eastern Kansas with clear skies\narea-wide, and without last night's cirrus, radiational cooling\nhas been more efficient. Temperatures are running several degrees\nbelow the HRRR, RAP, and RRFS, with crossover already met at\nTopeka and Manhattan, and road cameras confirm fog in the typical\nlow lying areas but nothing widespread or dense at this time.\nForecast soundings keep the saturated layer only tens of feet deep\nwith winds just above it increasing toward sunrise, so the fog\nmost likely remains patchy rather than widespread, and a light\nsoutherly breeze keeps areas around Emporia and southern portions\nof east central Kansas slightly mixed. Expect mainly patchy fog,\nlocally dense in the Kansas River valley, burning off by mid\nmorning.\n\nSunny and warm conditions in place today with highs in the low 70s\nunder the broad upper level ridge. A weak boundary sags into\nnorthern Kansas Tuesday, holding north central highs in the mid\n60s while the rest of the area stays near 70. Light winds and\nanother night of strong radiational cooling could allow patchy fog\nto redevelop Tuesday morning if the boundary layer decouples, so\ntrends in the 12Z guidance will need watching.\n\nThe cold front moves through early Wednesday with northwest winds\ngusting 25 to 30 mph in the afternoon. Behind it the trough\nsettles over the East with the ridge holding in the West, leaving\nKansas in northwest flow as H85 temperatures fall from near 11 C\nWednesday to near 0 C by Friday. The question for Thursday and\nFriday is how fast the western ridge builds back into the Plains.\nThe ECMWF and GEFS have come into better agreement holding the\ntrough axis over the East, which keeps Kansas in northwest flow\nwith the cold airmass in place through Friday. The GEPS builds the\nridge east faster, lifting heights and turning winds southerly by\nFriday, which cuts the CAA short giving reason for the warm tail in \nthe ensemble spread. With roughly 70 percent of members on the slower\nside, the forecast leans on the NBM median with highs in the low to \nmid 50s Thursday and around 50 Friday. Lows near 30 Friday and \nSaturday mornings bring possible frost and a freeze to much of the \narea after a growing season that has run long this fall without a \nhard freeze.\n\nRidging builds back over the Plains next weekend with highs\nclimbing through the 60s and dry weather holding through the\nperiod. Beyond that, some solutions advertise a western trough\nthat would bring the first rain chances back early the following\nweek.",
